const title       = 'How to Tell a Raven from a Crow in Flight — Key Field Marks';
const description = 'In flight, ravens differ from crows in tail shape (wedge vs fan), wing shape, wingbeat pattern, and size. Field identification guide for ravens vs crows in flight.';
const path        = '/questions/how-do-you-tell-a-raven-from-a-crow-in-flight/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'Raven vs crow in flight?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'How do you tell a raven from a crow in flight?', a: 'The most reliable in-flight field marks: (1) Tail shape — raven tail is wedge-shaped or diamond-shaped (central feathers projecting); crow tail is rounded or fan-shaped. (2) Size — ravens are substantially larger (wingspan 115–150 cm vs 90–105 cm for Carrion Crow). (3) Wing shape — ravens have proportionally longer, more "fingered" primaries. (4) Flight style — ravens soar and glide frequently; crows flap more continuously. (5) Wingbeat — raven wingbeats are slower and deeper.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} ogType="article"
  datePublished="2026-11-07" dateModified="2026-11-07"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} faq={faq} pageType="Article">

<article class="id-page">
  <nav class="id-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      {breadcrumbs.map((b, i) => (
        <li>
          {i < breadcrumbs.length - 1
            ? <><a href={b.url}>{b.name}</a><span aria-hidden="true"> › </span></>
            : <span aria-current="page">{b.name}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>
  <header class="id-header">
    <p class="id-pillar">Questions · Identification</p>
    <h1>How to Tell a Raven from a Crow in Flight</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Tail shape, wing shape, and flight style — the three keys</p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> In flight, focus on three things: <strong>(1) Tail shape</strong> — raven tail is wedge-shaped (central feathers project beyond outer ones); crow tail is fan- or round-ended. <strong>(2) Size</strong> — ravens are much larger (roughly the size of a buzzard). <strong>(3) Flight style</strong> — ravens soar and glide freely; crows flap more steadily. Any one of these is reliable; all three together give certainty.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>1. Tail Shape — The Most Reliable Mark</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Tail shape is visible at distance and is the single most reliable feature separating ravens from crows in flight:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Common Raven:</strong> Tail is distinctly wedge-shaped or diamond-shaped — the two central tail feathers are noticeably longer than the outer ones, producing a pointed central projection. This is visible from considerable distance in good light.</li>
      <li><strong>Carrion Crow / American Crow:</strong> Tail is rounded or fan-shaped — all feathers approximately equal length, producing a rounded tip.</li>
      <li><strong>Rook:</strong> Tail is also rounded (like crow), but the bird has a distinctive bare white face and "baggy trousers" feathering visible at closer range.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The wedge-shaped raven tail is consistent across subspecies and ages. It is the go-to identification feature for experienced observers.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>2. Size</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens are substantially larger than crows — but size is only useful when a reference is available:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Common Raven wingspan: 115–150 cm — comparable to a Buzzard</li>
      <li>Carrion Crow wingspan: 90–105 cm</li>
      <li>American Crow wingspan: 85–100 cm</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A raven soaring alongside a crow is immediately obviously larger. Alone against the sky, size is harder to judge without a reference. The "buzzard-sized all-black bird" observation is a good mental template for ravens.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>3. Flight Style</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens soar; crows flap:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Raven:</strong> Soars extensively on thermals and updraughts; glides freely; frequently holds wings flat or slightly raised in a shallow V; aerial acrobatics (rolling, diving) common, especially in pairs</li>
      <li><strong>Crow:</strong> Primarily flapping flight; soars briefly but typically returns to flapping; more purposeful, direct flight</li>
      <li><strong>Wingbeat:</strong> Raven wingbeats are slower, deeper, and more deliberate; crow wingbeats are faster and shallower</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A large all-black bird soaring in circles like a raptor is almost certainly a raven. A flock of black birds flapping steadily across a field is almost certainly crows (Rooks, Carrion Crows, or American Crows depending on location).<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Wing Shape</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> At close range, wing shape is also useful:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ravens have proportionally longer, more "fingered" primary feathers — the wing tip looks more splayed</li>
      <li>Crows have a neater, less "fingered" wing tip — primaries less deeply separated</li>
      <li>The raven's longer "fingers" are an adaptation for soaring flight (similar to raptors)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is a secondary feature — useful when visible, but tail shape, size, and flight style are more reliably distinguishable at distance.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Summary Table</h2>
    <div class="id-compare-table-wrap">
      <table class="id-compare-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Common Raven</th><th>Carrion Crow</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Tail shape</td><td>Wedge / diamond</td><td>Fan / rounded</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wingspan</td><td>115–150 cm</td><td>90–105 cm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Flight style</td><td>Soars freely; glides</td><td>Flaps steadily</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wingbeat</td><td>Slow, deep</td><td>Faster, shallower</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wing tip</td><td>Strongly fingered</td><td>Less fingered</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Call</td><td>Deep "kronk"</td><td>Harsh "kaa"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="id-faq id-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
